#include <vector>

long long total_volume(const std::vector<int>& fills) {
    long long total = 0;
    for (int f : fills) {
        total += f;
    }
    return total;
}

items.prompt:
A colleague ports a Python backtest to C++. Two results look wrong.

```cpp
int total_qty = 0;
for (int q : fills) {
    total_qty += q;
}
double avg = total_qty / n_fills;
```

On a day with two fills of 1,500,000,000 shares, the total prints −1,294,967,296. On a quieter day, 4 fills totalling 10,000,002 shares give `avg` as 2500000, not 2,500,000.5. Write them a short note. Say what C++ is doing in each case and why Python never showed it. Give a fix in code for each, and one way to catch each sooner.
